/**
\fn Point pdg::PhysicsConstraint::getAnchorA() const
\brief Read the connection point on body A.

Pin, slide, pivot and spring constraints support this getter. For a groove, use getGrooveStart() and getGrooveEnd() instead. Other constraint types throw. Coordinates are unscaled body-local
distance units, relative to getBodyA()'s physical frame, not a bone's joint
origin or the Sprite's frame. A retained disconnected constraint stays queryable.

\return A copied Point; changing it does not change the constraint.
\see setAnchorA(), setAnchors()
*/

/**
\fn Point pdg::PhysicsConstraint::getAnchorB() const
\brief Read the connection point on body B.

Pin, slide, pivot, groove and spring constraints support this getter. Other constraint types throw. Coordinates are unscaled body-local
distance units, relative to getBodyB()'s physical frame, not a bone's joint
origin or the Sprite's frame. A retained disconnected constraint stays queryable.

\return A copied Point; changing it does not change the constraint.
\see setAnchorB(), setAnchors()
*/

/**
\fn PhysicsConstraint& pdg::PhysicsConstraint::setAnchorA(const Point& anchor)
\brief Move the connection point on body A.

Pin, slide, pivot and spring constraints support this setter. For a groove, use setGroove() instead. Other constraint types throw. Finite coordinates are required;
invalid input leaves the constraint unchanged. Only this endpoint changes.
See setAnchors() for solver timing, geometry and lifetime behavior.

\param anchor New point in body A's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\return This constraint for chaining.
\see getAnchorA(), setAnchors()
*/

/**
\fn PhysicsConstraint& pdg::PhysicsConstraint::setAnchorB(const Point& anchor)
\brief Move the connection point on body B.

Pin, slide, pivot, groove and spring constraints support this setter. Other constraint types throw. Finite coordinates are required;
invalid input leaves the constraint unchanged. Only this endpoint changes.
See setAnchors() for solver timing, geometry and lifetime behavior.

\param anchor New point in body B's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\return This constraint for chaining.
\see getAnchorB(), setAnchors()
*/

/**
\fn PhysicsConstraint& pdg::PhysicsConstraint::setAnchors(const Point& anchorA, const Point& anchorB)
\brief Move both connection points atomically.

Supports pin, slide, pivot and spring constraints. Groove and angular-only
constraints throw. Both Points must have finite coordinates. Validation occurs
before either stored point changes; getters report successful edits immediately.

The next physics step uses the new anchors. This includes edits requested in a
collision callback: the active solver joint is not mutated during that solve.
Connected sleeping bodies wake when the edit reaches the native solver. Repeating
the same coordinates does not restart the joint. The public constraint handle,
body endpoints, force/break limits and collision policy are preserved.

Moving anchors does not teleport bodies, change velocities, resize colliders,
recalculate mass/inertia, or change a pin's distance, slide limits or spring rest
length. The solver may move bodies to satisfy the new geometry. Edited anchors
are saved in scene snapshots, including edits made before the next solve.
A retained disconnected constraint stays editable but is not reconnected.

This edits physical connection points, not animation bones or Part offsets.
Physical Part IK continues to validate its joint geometry against its Part chain;
incompatible anchors produce a controller error instead of silently moving its
captured chain offsets.

\param anchorA New point in body A's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\param anchorB New point in body B's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\return This constraint for chaining.

\note The example assumes upperArm and forearm are Parts with physics bodies
in the same layer, with millimeters as the application's distance unit. The
coordinates are relative to each body's origin.

\par Example
\code{.cpp}
auto& elbow = upperArm.physics.createPivotJoint(forearm.physics,
    pdg::Point(0, 150), pdg::Point(0, -130));
elbow.setAnchors(pdg::Point(0, 145), pdg::Point(0, -125));
\endcode

\see getAnchorA(), getAnchorB(), setAnchorA(), setAnchorB(), setGroove()
*/

/**
\fn Point pdg::PhysicsConstraint::getGrooveStart() const
\brief Read the start of a groove joint's track.

Only groove constraints support this getter; other types throw. Retained
disconnected grooves remain queryable.

\return A copied Point in body A's unscaled local distance units.
\see setGroove(), getAnchorB()
*/

/**
\fn Point pdg::PhysicsConstraint::getGrooveEnd() const
\brief Read the end of a groove joint's track.

Only groove constraints support this getter; other types throw. Retained
disconnected grooves remain queryable.

\return A copied Point in body A's unscaled local distance units.
\see setGroove(), getAnchorB()
*/

/**
\fn PhysicsConstraint& pdg::PhysicsConstraint::setGroove(const Point& start, const Point& end)
\brief Move both endpoints of a groove joint's track atomically.

Only groove constraints support this setter. Coordinates must be finite and the
endpoints more than 1e-8 local distance units apart. Invalid input leaves both
endpoints unchanged. Body B's anchor stays unchanged; use setAnchorB() to edit it.
Getters report the change immediately; the solver adopts it on the next step.
The same timing, lifetime and body-state rules as setAnchors() apply.

\param start New track start in body A's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\param end New track end in body A's unscaled local distance units; copied.
\return This constraint for chaining.
\see getGrooveStart(), getGrooveEnd(), setAnchorB(), setAnchors()
*/
